Z||000000||drednot||08-25-2001||02:39 PM||||I don't know what's wrong with this piece of **** !!!  I just changed the clutch and it still won't fully dis-engage!!!!!!!!!!!!!  I AM ABOUT TO SMASH THIS STUPID CAR WITH A HAMMER!!  ok,,, calm down.....  Now, I know it's not a hydraulic problem, so what else could it be??||||reg||1
Z||000001||88formula||08-25-2001||02:54 PM||||I feel your pain!<P>If your clutch slave cylinder does not travel at least an inch than your clutch won't fully disengage and you have a hydraulic problem. Other causes could be the transmission to bell housing bolts that are loose. A warped pressure plate or disc. Bent clutch fork or bent clutch pedal. Go to v8 Archie's website and read the article he wrote about this problem.<P>I'm having the same problem. It also seems that if the slave cylinder is not a GM slave than you will have problems.<BR><P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/88formula_logo_1.JPG"> ||||reg||1
Z||000002||starfighter007@msn.com||08-25-2001||02:57 PM||||I dont know what your original problem was that you made you decide to change out your clutch was. But you need to check your clutch pedal these are notorius for getting bent over time this is the first and easyiest thing to check first . And calm down go check your pedal now and get back  ||||reg||1
Z||000003||drednot||08-25-2001||04:28 PM||||I have the new steel pedal.  My original problem was the clutch not fully dis-engaging.  I have the correct travel @ the slave cly.  it goes all the way to the wire harnesses.  yet, the ****ing clutch won't dis-engage!!!!  I don't know, it is looking more and more like scrap metal to me.||||reg||1
